        -:  739: * represent raw memory, an array of POD elements, a vector of POD elements,
        -:  740: * or a std::string.
        -:  741: *
        -:  742: * A buffer object represents a contiguous region of memory as a 2-tuple
        -:  743: * consisting of a pointer and size in bytes. A tuple of the form <tt>{void*,
        -:  744: * size_t}</tt> specifies a mutable (modifiable) region of memory. Similarly, a
        -:  745: * tuple of the form <tt>{const void*, size_t}</tt> specifies a const
        -:  746: * (non-modifiable) region of memory. These two forms correspond to the classes
        -:  747: * mutable_buffer and const_buffer, respectively. To mirror C++'s conversion
        -:  748: * rules, a mutable_buffer is implicitly convertible to a const_buffer, and the
        -:  749: * opposite conversion is not permitted.
        -:  750: *
        -:  751: * The simplest use case involves reading or writing a single buffer of a
        -:  752: * specified size:
        -:  753: *
        -:  754: * @code sock.send(boost::asio::buffer(data, size)); @endcode
        -:  755: *
        -:  756: * In the above example, the return value of boost::asio::buffer meets the
        -:  757: * requirements of the ConstBufferSequence concept so that it may be directly
        -:  758: * passed to the socket's write function. A buffer created for modifiable
        -:  759: * memory also meets the requirements of the MutableBufferSequence concept.
        -:  760: *
        -:  761: * An individual buffer may be created from a builtin array, std::vector,
        -:  762: * std::array or boost::array of POD elements. This helps prevent buffer
        -:  763: * overruns by automatically determining the size of the buffer:
        -:  764: *
        -:  765: * @code char d1[128];
        -:  766: * size_t bytes_transferred = sock.receive(boost::asio::buffer(d1));
        -:  767: *
        -:  768: * std::vector<char> d2(128);
        -:  769: * bytes_transferred = sock.receive(boost::asio::buffer(d2));
        -:  770: *
        -:  771: * std::array<char, 128> d3;
        -:  772: * bytes_transferred = sock.receive(boost::asio::buffer(d3));
        -:  773: *
        -:  774: * boost::array<char, 128> d4;
        -:  775: * bytes_transferred = sock.receive(boost::asio::buffer(d4)); @endcode
        -:  776: *
        -:  777: * In all three cases above, the buffers created are exactly 128 bytes long.
        -:  778: * Note that a vector is @e never automatically resized when creating or using
        -:  779: * a buffer. The buffer size is determined using the vector's <tt>size()</tt>
        -:  780: * member function, and not its capacity.
        -:  781: *
        -:  782: * @par Accessing Buffer Contents
        -:  783: *
        -:  784: * The contents of a buffer may be accessed using the @c data() and @c size()
        -:  785: * member functions:
        -:  786: *
        -:  787: * @code boost::asio::mutable_buffer b1 = ...;
        -:  788: * std::size_t s1 = b1.size();
        -:  789: * unsigned char* p1 = static_cast<unsigned char*>(b1.data());
        -:  790: *
        -:  791: * boost::asio::const_buffer b2 = ...;
        -:  792: * std::size_t s2 = b2.size();
        -:  793: * const void* p2 = b2.data(); @endcode
        -:  794: *
        -:  795: * The @c data() member function permits violations of type safety, so
        -:  796: * uses of it in application code should be carefully considered.
        -:  797: *
        -:  798: * For convenience, a @ref buffer_size function is provided that works with
        -:  799: * both buffers and buffer sequences (that is, types meeting the
        -:  800: * ConstBufferSequence or MutableBufferSequence type requirements). In this
        -:  801: * case, the function returns the total size of all buffers in the sequence.
        -:  802: *
        -:  803: * @par Buffer Copying
        -:  804: *
        -:  805: * The @ref buffer_copy function may be used to copy raw bytes between
        -:  806: * individual buffers and buffer sequences.
        -:  807:*
        -:  808: * In particular, when used with the @ref buffer_size function, the @ref
        -:  809: * buffer_copy function can be used to linearise a sequence of buffers. For
        -:  810: * example:
        -:  811: *
        -:  812: * @code vector<const_buffer> buffers = ...;
        -:  813: *
        -:  814: * vector<unsigned char> data(boost::asio::buffer_size(buffers));
        -:  815: * boost::asio::buffer_copy(boost::asio::buffer(data), buffers); @endcode
        -:  816: *
        -:  817: * Note that @ref buffer_copy is implemented in terms of @c memcpy, and
        -:  818: * consequently it cannot be used to copy between overlapping memory regions.
        -:  819: *
        -:  820: * @par Buffer Invalidation
        -:  821: *
        -:  822: * A buffer object does not have any ownership of the memory it refers to. It
        -:  823: * is the responsibility of the application to ensure the memory region remains
        -:  824: * valid until it is no longer required for an I/O operation. When the memory
        -:  825: * is no longer available, the buffer is said to have been invalidated.
        -:  826: *
        -:  827: * For the boost::asio::buffer overloads that accept an argument of type
        -:  828: * std::vector, the buffer objects returned are invalidated by any vector
        -:  829: * operation that also invalidates all references, pointers and iterators
        -:  830: * referring to the elements in the sequence (C++ Std, 23.2.4)
        -:  831: *
        -:  832: * For the boost::asio::buffer overloads that accept an argument of type
        -:  833: * std::basic_string, the buffer objects returned are invalidated according to
        -:  834: * the rules defined for invalidation of references, pointers and iterators
        -:  835: * referring to elements of the sequence (C++ Std, 21.3).
        -:  836: *
        -:  837: * @par Buffer Arithmetic
        -:  838: *
        -:  839: * Buffer objects may be manipulated using simple arithmetic in a safe way
        -:  840: * which helps prevent buffer overruns. Consider an array initialised as
        -:  841: * follows:
        -:  842: *
        -:  843: * @code boost::array<char, 6> a = { 'a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e' }; @endcode
        -:  844: *
        -:  845: * A buffer object @c b1 created using:
        -:  846: *
        -:  847: * @code b1 = boost::asio::buffer(a); @endcode
        -:  848: *
        -:  849: * represents the entire array, <tt>{ 'a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e' }</tt>. An
        -:  850: * optional second argument to the boost::asio::buffer function may be used to
        -:  851: * limit the size, in bytes, of the buffer:
        -:  852: *
        -:  853: * @code b2 = boost::asio::buffer(a, 3); @endcode
        -:  854: *
        -:  855: * such that @c b2 represents the data <tt>{ 'a', 'b', 'c' }</tt>. Even if the
        -:  856: * size argument exceeds the actual size of the array, the size of the buffer
        -:  857: * object created will be limited to the array size.
        -:  858: *
        -:  859: * An offset may be applied to an existing buffer to create a new one:
        -:  860: *
        -:  861: * @code b3 = b1 + 2; @endcode
        -:  862: *
        -:  863: * where @c b3 will set to represent <tt>{ 'c', 'd', 'e' }</tt>. If the offset
        -:  864: * exceeds the size of the existing buffer, the newly created buffer will be
        -:  865: * empty.
        -:  866: *
        -:  867: * Both an offset and size may be specified to create a buffer that corresponds
        -:  868: * to a specific range of bytes within an existing buffer:
        -:  869: *
        -:  870: * @code b4 = boost::asio::buffer(b1 + 1, 3); @endcode
        -:  871: *
        -:  872: * so that @c b4 will refer to the bytes <tt>{ 'b', 'c', 'd' }</tt>.
        -:  873: *
        -:  874: * @par Buffers and Scatter-Gather I/O
        -:  875: *
        -:  876: * To read or write using multiple buffers (i.e. scatter-gather I/O), multiple
        -:  877: * buffer objects may be assigned into a container that supports the
        -:  878: * MutableBufferSequence (for read) or ConstBufferSequence (for write) concepts:
        -:  879: *
        -:  880: * @code
        -:  881: * char d1[128];
        -:  882: * std::vector<char> d2(128);
        -:  883: * boost::array<char, 128> d3;
        -:  884: *
        -:  885: * boost::array<mutable_buffer, 3> bufs1 = {
        -:  886: *   boost::asio::buffer(d1),
        -:  887: *   boost::asio::buffer(d2),
        -:  888: *   boost::asio::buffer(d3) };
        -:  889: * bytes_transferred = sock.receive(bufs1);
        -:  890: *
        -:  891: * std::vector<const_buffer> bufs2;
        -:  892: * bufs2.push_back(boost::asio::buffer(d1));
        -:  893: * bufs2.push_back(boost::asio::buffer(d2));
        -:  894: * bufs2.push_back(boost::asio::buffer(d3));
        -:  895: * bytes_transferred = sock.send(bufs2); @endcode
        -:  896: *
        -:  897: * @par Buffer Literals
        -:  898: *
        -:  899: * The `_buf` literal suffix, defined in namespace
        -:  900: * <tt>boost::asio::buffer_literals</tt>, may be used to create @c const_buffer
        -:  901: * objects from string, binary integer, and hexadecimal integer literals.
        -:  902: * For example:
        -:  903: *
        -:  904: * @code
        -:  905: * using namespace boost::asio::buffer_literals;
        -:  906: *
        -:  907: * boost::asio::const_buffer b1 = "hello"_buf;
        -:  908: * boost::asio::const_buffer b2 = 0xdeadbeef_buf;
        -:  909: * boost::asio::const_buffer b3 = 0x0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef_buf;
        -:  910: * boost::asio::const_buffer b4 = 0b1010101011001100_buf; @endcode
        -:  911: *
        -:  912: * Note that the memory associated with a buffer literal is valid for the
        -:  913: * lifetime of the program. This means that the buffer can be safely used with
        -:  914: * asynchronous operations.
        -:  915: */
        -:  916:/*@{*/
